== The Parents’ Duty towards Their Children

=== §. 115. #emph[Of the connection of parents’ duty with children’s.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 6:4.]

    #text(style: "italic")[And you#super[+] fathers, do not provoke your#super[+] children to wrath, but bring them up in the nurture and admonition of the Lord.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[The] apostle, having urged children to perform their duties to their parents, turns his speech to parents, saying, “AND you#super[+] fathers,” etc. That joining word AND, which joins an exhortation to parents to perform their duties to the exhortation made to children before it, gives us to understand that

#emph[Parents are bound to duty as well as children.] Their duties #footnote[1\. #emph[Observation.] Parents bound to duties.] are indeed different; yet (notwithstanding their superiority and authority over their children) they are bound to duty. All the directions and exhortations throughout Scripture given to parents concerning their duty, and all the threatenings pronounced and judgments executed on parents for neglecting their duty, are clear proofs of this point.

Though parents are over their children and cannot be commanded by them, yet they are under God, and it is he who has laid their duty on them. So they are bound to it, as they will answer for it to their Father in heaven.

The authority which parents have is not so much for their own advancement as for the better governing of their children. That being so, their very government is a duty.

#emph[Objection.] In the moral law only the duty of children is expressed. #footnote[How parents’ duties are implied in the fifth commandment.]

#emph[Answer.] The duty of parents (like the duties of many others) is implied by just and necessary consequence, which is equivalent and binds as much as if it were expressed. It is implied in this way. Those who have honor must carry themselves worthy of honor. Now the way to carry themselves worthy of honor is to be careful in doing their duty to those who honor them. This is so fair that it needed no expressing.

Therefore let ministers follow this pattern of the apostle, #footnote[1\. #emph[Use.] Ministers must stir up parents to their duty.] and deal evenly with all of every sort. Let them not be partial, laying all the burden of duty on children’s necks and none on parents, holding children in very strictly but leaving parents to their own will. Parents are flesh and blood as well as children, and as prone to transgress in their place as children in theirs. Indeed, of the two, ministers ought to be more earnest in urging parents to perform their duty, because parents are under no such power and authority as children are. Fear of their parents’ authority keeps children much in awe. There is no such thing to keep parents in awe. They will therefore be all the readier to take the greater liberty, if they are not kept within bounds by the fear of God and by a good conscience.

Now you#super[+] parents, as you#super[+] look for honor, carry yourselves#super[+] #footnote[2\. #emph[Use.]] worthy of honor; as you#super[+] look for duty from your#super[+] children, perform duty to them. Know that one day you#super[+] too will be called to account before the highest Judge. Your#super[+] authority will then be no pretext to excuse your#super[+] fault, but evidence to aggravate it. For you#super[+], being older in years and more eminent in place, of more experience, and having a charge over your#super[+] children, ought to be a light to show them the way and an example to draw them on. Then, seeing you#super[+] careful and conscientious in performing your#super[+] duty, they may be the more stirred up to perform theirs, or at least be made ashamed of neglecting their duty. But if you#super[+] are careless of your#super[+] duty, how can you#super[+] expect duty at their hands? Indeed, if by your#super[+] bad example they have been made negligent, their blood will be required at your#super[+] hands.

=== §. 116. #emph[Of the extent of these words,] Fathers, Children.

The word #emph[(fathers)] is used here, which properly #footnote[πατέρες.] means #emph[natural parents,] and of natural parents the #emph[male sex.] Yet (as in many other places) it is to be taken more widely: as widely as the word #emph[(children)] was taken before, so that there may be a just and equal relation between children #footnote[#emph[v.] 1.] and parents. So both sexes of natural parents are included under it, #emph[mother] as well as #emph[father.] So also are those who are in the place of parents, whether by #emph[marriage,] as stepparents and all sorts of fathers- and mothers-in-law, or #emph[by appointment,] as all who by right have the custody and charge of children, such as guardians, tutors, and other such governors. So it answers in every way to the word #emph[(parents)] used in the first verse; and the word #emph[(children)] is also to be taken here in the same extent as it was there.

=== §. 117. #emph[Of parents provoking children.]

The next phrase #emph[(provoke to wrath)] is the rendering of one #footnote[παροργίζετε.] Greek word, which, being a compound word, cannot be fully expressed by one English word. The best and nearest that I can think of is (exasperate). The word signifies an extreme in the use of authority: too much austerity and severity, by which children are provoked to wrath. Because that is a fault, it is expressly forbidden here #emph[(do not provoke, etc.)] There is a figure of speech in this word: the #emph[effect] is put for the #emph[cause.] The apostle’s meaning is that parents should take such care over their conduct toward their children that they give them no occasion to be stirred up to wrath. So under this word are forbidden #footnote[Occasions of provoking children to wrath.] all such things as may kindle wrath in children: too much austerity in conduct, sourness in look, threatening and reviling in words, too hard handling, too severe correction, too much restraint of liberty, too small an allowance of things needful, and the like. Parents, being flesh and blood, are prone in this respect to abuse their authority. Indeed, even those who fall into the other extreme, of too much indulgence and pampering of their children, are very prone to fall into this extreme also. Many who for the most part let their children run into all kinds of wild living, without due restraint and correction, will at times suddenly fly at them like lions and correct them “at their own pleasure,” and so greatly provoke #footnote[Heb 12:10.] their children. Those who pamper the most are the most prone to provoke to wrath. For, 1. such parents least know how to keep the middle way: one will sooner leap out of one extreme into the other than go from an extreme to the middle way. 2. The children of such parents are the soonest provoked.

#emph[Question.] Is it lawful and justifiable for children to be provoked to wrath by their parents?

#emph[Answer.] #footnote[See Treatise 5, §. 31, 41.] No. This prohibition intends no such thing. The apostle is dealing here with parents, and instructs them how to prevent such harms as their children may fall into through their weakness. So here he shows only what is unlawful for parents, not what is lawful for children. From this, then, I observe by the way that

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Parents must be so watchful over their conduct that by it they] #footnote[2\. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[do not make their children sin.]]

If they do, they make their own sin the more heinous. They also pull down on their own heads a far heavier vengeance: the vengeance of their own sin, and the vengeance of their child’s sin. For every parent is made a watchman over his child. If a watchman#footnote[Ezek 3:18.] does not do what he can to hinder the sin of those under his charge, he brings their blood on his own neck. What then do those do who, being watchmen, give occasion of sin to those under their charge?

=== §. 118. #emph[Of parents seeking the good of their children.]

So that parents, in avoiding the rock of #emph[provoking,] do not fall into the gulf of #emph[pampering,] the apostle adds a BUT, which is #footnote[ἀλλά.] as a check to them, and teaches them that

#emph[It is not enough for parents to prevent such harms as] #footnote[3\. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[their children may fall into; they must also seek their good.] All the precepts in Scripture charging parents to seek their children’s good prove the point. Here lies a chief difference between the affection which parents and strangers ought to bear toward children, and the duty which each owes to them. Mere strangers ought not to provoke them; but parents ought, moreover, to seek their good in every way.

The main good which parents ought especially to seek for their children is set out in these words: “Bring them up in the nurture and admonition of the Lord.”

The word translated #emph[(bring up)] properly means to feed #footnote[ἐκτρέφετε.] or nourish with all needful things. It is the same word used before in chapter 5, verse 29, and translated there #emph[nourishes.] It would not be unfitting to keep the proper meaning of the #footnote[ἐκτρέφει. #emph[Enutrite,] #emph[Beza.]] word here, as the best Latin translations, the French, and others have kept it.

This word, joined with the others that follow, may seem #footnote[Nourrissez. Fr.] at first sight to be placed here only to fill out the sense, as if he had said, #emph[nurture your child in the ways of God.] But if the apostle’s aim and the meaning of the word are well weighed, we shall find that it further implies a general duty, which nature itself teaches parents, namely this, that

#emph[Parents ought to provide all needful things for their children:] #footnote[4\. #emph[Observation.] Parents to provide all needful things for children. ἐκτρέφετε αὐτὰ ἐν παιδείᾳ. ἐκτρέφετε καὶ παιδεύετε αὐτά.] that is, such things as serve to nourish their bodies and preserve their health and life. For this phrase (to translate it word for word), #emph[nourish them in discipline,] or in instruction, is a concise expression implying as much as if he had said, #emph[nourish and nurture them,] or #emph[feed and instruct them.] But the apostle has joined them together this closely and concisely to show that #emph[nurture and instruction are as needful and profitable as food and clothing.]

=== §. 119. #emph[Of parents nurturing their children.] #footnote[5\. #emph[Observation.] παιδεία. εἰ παιδείαν ὑπομένετε. πρὸς παιδείαν τὴν ἐν δικαιοσύνῃ.]

The word translated #emph[nurture] signifies #emph[correction] as well as #emph[instruction:] as in Heb 12:7, “If you#super[+] endure chastening,” and 2 Tim 3:16, Scripture is profitable “for instruction in righteousness.” Both senses will stand here, and our English word (as well as the Greek) will bear both: for to #emph[nurture] children is to #emph[correct] them as well as to #emph[instruct] them. This advice, in this wide sense, very fitly follows the prohibition before it. For lest parents should take occasion from that to lay the reins on their children’s necks and let them run wherever they please, the apostle teaches by this that

#emph[Parents, as they must not be too severe, so neither must they be too lax.] #footnote[6\. #emph[Observation.] παιδεία.] They must not provoke their children to wrath, yet they must keep them under discipline. The word translated #emph[nurture,] by the #footnote[παιδεύειν, #emph[Instituere vt puerili aetati conuenit.]] derivation of the Greek, further sets out the middle way between the two extremes named before. For it signifies #emph[such discipline as befits a boy, or a young child.] So the thing itself, #emph[discipline,] by instruction and correction, keeps parents from the one extreme of #emph[laxness;] and the kind or #emph[manner] of discipline, being such as befits a child, keeps them from the other extreme of #emph[rigor] and cruelty. Extremes on either side are dangerous and harmful, and that to both parent and child. For #emph[laxness] will make children careless of all duty to God and parent; #emph[rigor] will make them desperate. But virtue and safety lie in the middle way between both.

=== §. 120. #emph[Of parents fixing precepts in their children’s minds.]

This word #emph[(admonition),] by its #footnote[νουθεσία. νουθετεῖν, #emph[menti indere.] \[to put into the mind\]] derivation, has a particular relation to the #emph[mind,] and points to the informing and instructing of it. It is taken either for the act of admonishing (as in Titus 3:10, “Reject a divisive man #footnote[μετὰ μίαν καὶ δευτέραν νουθεσίαν.] after a first and second admonition”), or for the #footnote[In monitis.] thing admonished, which is the sense most take it in here. Yet I would not have the former wholly excluded; for, by the full meaning of the word, I take this much to be intended.

#emph[As parents deliver good precepts and principles to their children,] #footnote[7\. #emph[Observation.] νουθετεῖν.] #emph[so they must take care, by forceful and frequent admonitions, to fix and settle them in their children’s minds.] #footnote[Deut 6:7.] The law expresses as much by another metaphor which it uses, in a direction it gives to parents, saying, “You shall whet, or sharpen, God’s laws upon your children,” that is, #footnote[#emph[Doctus inter Hebr. vocem] שנן continuò loqui #emph[exponit, (i.) inculcare, vel identidem repetere. Innuit studium & diligentiam qua pueris praecepta Dei inculcari debent.] \[A learned man among the Hebrews explains the word שנן as “to speak continually,” that is, to drive in, or to repeat again and again. It points to the zeal and diligence with which God’s precepts ought to be driven into children.\] #emph[Vatabl. in] Deut 6:7. See #emph[Treatise] 6, §. 42.] “you shall teach them diligently to your children.”

The more pains are taken in this, the less labor will be lost. What is little heeded at first will, by much urging and pressing, be held forever, as a nail that scarcely enters at one blow is driven in up to the head with many blows.

=== §. 121. #emph[Of adding information to discipline.]

The addition of this word #emph[admonition] to #emph[nurture] is not (as some take it) a mere explanation of the same point, but also a declaration of a further duty, which is this:

#emph[As parents by discipline keep their children in check, so by information] #footnote[8\. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[they must direct them in the right way.] Solomon both delivers the point and adds a good reason to enforce it. For he says, “Train up a child in the way he should go”: there is the duty. “And when he is old he will not depart from it”#footnote[Prov 22:6.]: there is the reason. Keeping a child in check by good discipline may make him dutiful while his father is over him; but informing his understanding and judgment well is a means to uphold him in the right way as long as he lives.

=== §. 122. #emph[Of parents teaching their children the fear of God.]

The last word #emph[(of the Lord)] points to the best duty that #footnote[Κυρίου.] a parent can do for his child. #emph[Admonition of the Lord] means such principles as a parent has received from the Lord and learned out of God’s word: such as may teach a child to fear the Lord, such as tend to true piety and religion. From this I further observe that

#emph[Parents must especially teach their children their duty to] #footnote[9\. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[God.] “Come, children,” says the psalmist, “listen to me; I will teach you#super[+] the fear of the #smallcaps[Lord].” I will speak of this particular more fully #footnote[Treatise 6, §. 34.] hereafter.
